<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="chapter-algebra-factorisation">
  <title>Factorisation</title>
  <section xml:id="algebra-factorisation-intro">
    <title>Introduction</title>
    <introduction><p>To factorise an expression is to rewrite it as a product. Factorisation reverses expansion. A common factor can be taken outside brackets; other patterns require recognising how products expand.</p></introduction>
    <p><em>Common factors first.</em> Examine the terms for a common factor before attempting any other factorisation method. If a non-unit HCF exists, extract it first. For example, <m>24x^2+96x+90=6(4x^2+16x+15)=6(2x+3)(2x+5)</m>. Unless otherwise stated, our subsequent positive-integer trinomial investigations assume that the HCF of the coefficients is 1.</p>
    <p>For teachers: if <m>\gcd(a,b)=\gcd(c,d)=1</m>, the product <m>(ax+b)(cx+d)=Ax^2+Bx+C</m> has <m>\gcd(A,B,C)=1</m>. This is the primitive-polynomial form of Gauss's lemma. Removing common factors first therefore gives a mathematically consistent starting point in both directions.</p>
    <p>Always check a factorisation by expanding the result. A correct answer must reproduce every term of the original expression.</p>
    <paragraphs xml:id="algebra-guide-juggle-numbers"><title>Guide number and juggle numbers</title>
    <p>After checking for a common factor, consider a positive-coefficient trinomial <m>Ax^2+Bx+C</m>. If it arises from <m>(ax+b)(cx+d)</m>, then <m>A=ac</m>, <m>B=ad+bc</m> and <m>C=bd</m>. We call the two cross-product coefficients <m>ad</m> and <m>bc</m> the <term>juggle numbers</term><idx>juggle numbers</idx>. Their product is <m>(ad)(bc)=AC</m>, which we call the <term>guide number</term><idx>guide number</idx>.</p>
    <p>The guide number guides the search: we seek two numbers whose product is <m>AC</m> and whose sum is <m>B</m>. We are interested in the pair that meets both conditions. A complete prime factorisation of the guide number is not a necessary step; use familiar products, systematic factor pairs or another efficient strategy. The guide number is a consequence of the structure, not an unexplained rule.</p>
    </paragraphs>
    <paragraphs xml:id="algebra-factorisation-metacognition"><title>Learning to Learn — Working Backwards</title>
    <p>Expansion and factorisation are not unrelated skills. Recognising their shared structure helps build a reusable algebraic schema. Working backwards is one of the heuristics explored by Pólya. See <xref ref="problem-solving-heuristics" text="title"/>.</p>
    <example xml:id="factorisation-polya-example"><title>From an unknown factorisation to a familiar structure</title>
    <statement><p>Factorise <m>4x^2+15x+9</m>.</p></statement>
    <solution>
    <p><em>Understand.</em> The HCF is 1. We seek two linear factors with integer coefficients. Their cross-products must add to 15 and multiply to <m>4\cdot9=36</m>.</p>
    <p><em>Plan.</em> The guide number is <m>36</m>. Find two juggle numbers with product 36 and sum 15. Here <m>3\cdot12=36</m> and <m>3+12=15</m>. A systematic search of factor pairs is available if we cannot recognise the pair immediately.</p>
    <p><em>Carry out the plan.</em> Split the middle term and group:</p>
    <me>4x^2+12x+3x+9=4x(x+3)+3(x+3)=(4x+3)(x+3).</me>
    <p><em>Look back.</em> Expand to check. Why did the product and sum conditions help? Could you reuse the strategy for another positive trinomial? What would change if the middle coefficient were negative?</p>
    </solution></example>
    <p>The aim is not only to obtain an answer. It is to recognise a structure and learn how to approach the next unfamiliar problem independently.</p>
    </paragraphs>
    <p>For a paired expansion and factorisation investigation, begin with <xref ref="af3-01-positive-factors-unit-factor"/>. Recognise the same structure in both directions before moving to more general trinomials.</p>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="algebra-grouping-friendly-pairs">
    <title>Factorisation by Grouping — Friendly Pairs</title>
    <objectives><title>Learning Intentions</title><ol>
      <li><p>Recognise which partial products share a factor.</p></li>
      <li><p>Choose friendly pairs when factorising by grouping.</p></li>
    </ol></objectives>
    <p>Start with two factors, <m>(a+b)(c+d)</m>. Recall the firsts, inners, outers and lasts from expansion. Look at the two original terms used to make each product.</p>
    <me>\begin{array}{c|c|c|c}
    \text{Firsts}&amp;\text{Inners}&amp;\text{Outers}&amp;\text{Lasts}\\
    ac&amp;bc&amp;ad&amp;bd
    \end{array}</me>
    <paragraphs xml:id="algebra-grouping-avoid-opposites"><title>Which pair should I avoid?</title>
      <p><em>If you have the inners, do not pair them with the outers. If you have the firsts, do not pair them with the lasts. The same applies in reverse. Choose either of the other products.</em></p>
      <me>\begin{array}{ccccc}
      \text{Inners}&amp;bc&amp;\quad\not\leftrightarrow\quad&amp;ad&amp;\text{Outers}\\[4pt]
      \text{Firsts}&amp;ac&amp;\quad\not\leftrightarrow\quad&amp;bd&amp;\text{Lasts}
      \end{array}</me>
      <p>The inner and outer arcs have no endpoints in common. The first and last products likewise use different pairs of original terms. Neither pairing is guaranteed to have a common original factor.</p>
      <p>Every other pairing shares one of the original terms. We call these <term>friendly pairs</term><idx>friendly pairs</idx>. Pair either extreme (firsts or lasts) with either arc (inners or outers).</p>
      <me>\begin{array}{c|cc}
      &amp;\text{Inners }bc&amp;\text{Outers }ad\\ \hline
      \text{Firsts }ac&amp;c&amp;a\\
      \text{Lasts }bd&amp;b&amp;d
      \end{array}</me>
      <p>Each entry in the table is the shared original factor. Once one friendly pair is chosen, the remaining two products form the other friendly pair. Factoring the two pairs exposes a common binomial.</p>
      <me>\begin{aligned}
      (ac+bc)+(ad+bd)&amp;=c(a+b)+d(a+b)\\
      &amp;=(a+b)(c+d).
      \end{aligned}</me>
      <me>\begin{aligned}
      (ac+ad)+(bc+bd)&amp;=a(c+d)+b(c+d)\\
      &amp;=(a+b)(c+d).
      \end{aligned}</me>
      <p>These are the two productive groupings of the four labelled products. The remaining pairing is generally unproductive, although particular values or expressions can introduce additional common factors. The order of the terms does not change the expanded expression.</p>
    </paragraphs>
    <example xml:id="algebra-grouping-deceptive-pairs"><title>When both unfriendly pairs have common factors</title>
      <statement><p>Factorise <m>12xy+15+18y+10x</m>. Try the first two and last two terms before rearranging anything.</p></statement>
      <solution><me>12xy+15+18y+10x=3(4xy+5)+2(9y+5x).</me>
      <p>Both pairs have a common factor. The extraction is correct, but the binomials are different. This grouping does not expose the intended factorisation. Go back and swap the middle terms, retaining their signs.</p>
      <me>\begin{aligned}
      12xy+18y+15+10x&amp;=6y(2x+3)+5(3+2x)\\
      &amp;=6y(2x+3)+5(2x+3)\\
      &amp;=(2x+3)(6y+5).
      \end{aligned}</me>
      <p>Finding common factors within both pairs is not enough. Look for a common binomial after extraction.</p></solution>
    </example>
    <exercise xml:id="algebra-grouping-create-pairs"><title>Create your own grouping questions</title>
      <statement><p>Expand <m>(a+2)(ab+2)</m>. Write the four partial products in a friendly grouping and in an unfriendly grouping. Exchange your questions with a partner and factorise. Can an unfriendly pair still have a common factor?</p></statement>
      <answer><p>The partial products are <m>a^2b,2ab,2a,4</m>. One friendly grouping is <m>(a^2b+2ab)+(2a+4)</m>, which gives <m>ab(a+2)+2(a+2)</m>. The unfriendly grouping <m>(a^2b+4)+(2ab+2a)</m> includes a pair with common factor <m>2a</m>, despite the original products sharing no labelled source term.</p></answer>
    </exercise>
    <example xml:id="algebra-grouping-friendly-example"><title>Choose friendly pairs</title>
      <statement><p>Factorise <m>12y+10x+8xy+15</m>.</p></statement>
      <solution>
        <p>In this example the first two terms are the inner and outer products. Rearrange to form friendly pairs:</p>
        <me>\begin{aligned}
        12y+10x+8xy+15&amp;=(8xy+12y)+(10x+15)\\
        &amp;=4y(2x+3)+5(2x+3)\\
        &amp;=(2x+3)(4y+5).
        \end{aligned}</me>
        <p>Expand to check. Can you find the other productive grouping?</p>
      </solution>
    </example>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="algebra-special-cases-overview">
    <title>Special Cases at a Glance</title>
    <objectives><title>Learning Intentions</title><ol><li><p>Recognise special cases of the general expansion.</p></li><li><p>Distinguish same-sign and mixed-sign patterns and reverse identities.</p></li></ol></objectives>
    <p>Start with the general identity <m>(ax+b)(cx+d)\equiv acx^2+(ad+bc)x+bd</m>. In <m>Ax^2+Bx+C</m>, <m>A</m> is the quadratic coefficient, <m>B</m> is the linear coefficient and <m>C</m> is the constant term. In the special cases below, <m>a,b</m> are positive integers. Primes can be convenient examples, but primality is not required. The quadratic coefficient is <m>a</m> in every row.</p>
    <p><em>Same signs.</em></p>
    <me>\begin{array}{c|c|c}
    \text{Factors}&amp;B&amp;C\\\hline
    (ax+b)(x+1)&amp;a+b&amp;b\\
    (ax-b)(x-1)&amp;-(a+b)&amp;b\\
    (ax+1)(x+b)&amp;ab+1&amp;b\\
    (ax-1)(x-b)&amp;-(ab+1)&amp;b
    \end{array}</me>
    <p><em>Mixed signs.</em></p>
    <me>\begin{array}{c|c|c}
    \text{Factors}&amp;B&amp;C\\\hline
    (ax+b)(x-1)&amp;b-a&amp;-b\\
    (ax-b)(x+1)&amp;a-b&amp;-b\\
    (ax+1)(x-b)&amp;1-ab&amp;-b\\
    (ax-1)(x+b)&amp;ab-1&amp;-b
    \end{array}</me>
    <p>Expand, observe, identify a pattern, establish the identity, then reverse it to factorise. Use the inner and outer arcs to identify the two contributions to the linear coefficient. These recognition rules apply to the stated special cases, not to arbitrary trinomials.</p>
    <example xml:id="algebra-special-cases-deterministic-placement"><title>When a shortcut does not apply</title>
      <statement><p>Factorise <m>2x^2+7x+6</m>, given that the constants in the two brackets are 2 and 3.</p></statement>
      <solution><p>Neither the sum-centre nor product-plus-one pattern applies: <m>2+6\ne7</m> and <m>2\cdot6+1\ne7</m>. The HCF of the coefficients is 1. Placing the constant 2 with <m>2x</m> would produce a factor of 2 throughout the resulting polynomial, so the constant 2 must instead be placed with <m>x</m>.</p>
      <me>\begin{aligned}
      2x^2+7x+6&amp;=2x^2+4x+3x+6\\
      &amp;=2x(x+2)+3(x+2)\\
      &amp;=(2x+3)(x+2).
      \end{aligned}</me>
      <p>With these constants supplied, their placement is determined, not guessed. Later, use the guide number and juggle numbers to find the split independently. Check by expanding.</p></solution>
    </example>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="af3-factorisation-revisit">
<title>AF3-01: Revisit, Recognise, Reverse</title>
<objectives><title>Learning Intentions</title><ol>
<li><p>Recognise and use the sum-centre pattern.</p></li>
<li><p>Recognise and use the product-plus-one centre pattern.</p></li>
<li><p>Write a factorisation as an identity and check it by expanding.</p></li>
</ol></objectives>
<paragraphs xml:id="af2-factorisation-signpost"><title>Curriculum signpost — AF2: Algebraic expressions</title><p><em>Interpret, substitute, understand equality.</em> Return to familiar expressions with a new purpose. Recognise the coefficients, check selected numerical values and explain why the factorised expression is equal to the original for every permitted input. AF3 concerns the transformation itself; AF7 provides a graphical connection.</p><p>Revisit the <xref ref="af2-expansion-signpost" text="title"/> for the original curriculum connection.</p></paragraphs>
<paragraphs xml:id="af3-revisit-learning-to-learn"><title>Learning to Learn — Working Backwards</title>
<p>Factorisation is the inverse operation of expanding brackets. If we recognise a pattern when expanding brackets, we can use that pattern to reverse the operation. We begin with two special cases involving positive coefficients. Revisit <xref ref="af3-01-positive-factors-unit-factor"/> to see the original expansion using the inline, display and area-model representations.</p>
</paragraphs>
<definition xml:id="af3-positive-unit-factor"><title>Positive unit factor</title>
<statement><p>We call <m>x+1</m> a <term>positive unit factor</term><idx>positive unit factor</idx><idx>unit factor</idx> because both the coefficient of <m>x</m> and the constant term are positive units:</p>
<me>\underbrace{(1x+1)}_{\text{positive unit factor}}.</me></statement>
</definition>
<p>In the trinomial <m>Ax^2+Bx+C</m>, we call <m>A</m> and <m>C</m> the <term>end coefficients</term><idx>end coefficients</idx> and <m>B</m> the <term>centre coefficient</term><idx>centre coefficient</idx>. The formal names are the leading coefficient, linear coefficient and constant term, respectively for <m>A</m>, <m>B</m> and <m>C</m>.</p>
<paragraphs xml:id="af3-sum-centre-pattern"><title>1. Sum-centre pattern</title>
<p>Both units belong to the same factor, <m>x+1</m>.</p>
<p><em>In words.</em> The sum of the end coefficients equals the centre coefficient.</p>
<me>\underbrace{A}_{\text{first}}+\underbrace{C}_{\text{last}}=\underbrace{B}_{\text{centre}}.</me>
<p><em>Symbolically.</em><idx>sum-centre pattern</idx></p>
<me>\boxed{Ax^2+(A+C)x+C\equiv(Ax+C)(x+1)}</me>
</paragraphs>
<example xml:id="af3-revisit-friends-twenty"><title>Revisit — expansion</title>
<statement><p>Expand <m>(9x+11)(x+1)</m>.</p></statement>
<solution><me>\begin{aligned}(9x+11)(x+1)&amp;\equiv9x^2+9x+11x+11\\&amp;\equiv9x^2+20x+11.\end{aligned}</me>
<p>Notice that <m>9+11=20</m>.</p></solution></example>
<example xml:id="af3-revisit-reverse-thirty"><title>Recognise and reverse — factorisation</title>
<statement><p>Factorise <m>13x^2+30x+17</m>.</p></statement>
<solution><p><em>Recognise.</em> The end coefficients add to the centre coefficient: <m>13+17=30</m>.</p>
<p><em>Reverse.</em></p><me>\boxed{13x^2+30x+17\equiv(13x+17)(x+1)}</me>
<p>Expand to check all three coefficients.</p></solution></example>
<exercise xml:id="af3-revisit-paired-practice"><title>Sum-centre practice</title>
  <statement><p>Factorise each expression by recognising the pattern. Write your answer as an identity.</p><ol>
    <li><p><m>3x^2+11x+8</m></p></li>
    <li><p><m>7x^2+12x+5</m></p></li>
    <li><p><m>19x^2+30x+11</m></p></li>
    <li><p><m>17x^2+100x+83</m></p></li>
    <li><p><m>ax^2+(a+b)x+b</m></p></li>
    <li><p><m>a^2x^2+(a^2+b)x+b</m></p></li>
  </ol></statement>
  <answer><ol>
    <li><p><m>3x^2+11x+8\equiv(3x+8)(x+1)</m></p></li>
    <li><p><m>7x^2+12x+5\equiv(7x+5)(x+1)</m></p></li>
    <li><p><m>19x^2+30x+11\equiv(19x+11)(x+1)</m></p></li>
    <li><p><m>17x^2+100x+83\equiv(17x+83)(x+1)</m></p></li>
    <li><p><m>ax^2+(a+b)x+b\equiv(ax+b)(x+1)</m></p></li>
    <li><p><m>a^2x^2+(a^2+b)x+b\equiv(a^2x+b)(x+1)</m></p></li>
  </ol></answer>
</exercise>
<paragraphs xml:id="af3-product-plus-one-centre-pattern"><title>2. Product-plus-one centre pattern</title>
<p>We now place one positive unit in each factor, rather than both in the same factor.</p>
<p><em>In words.</em> The product of the end coefficients, increased by one, equals the centre coefficient.</p>
<me>\underbrace{A\cdot C}_{\text{product of ends}}+1=\underbrace{B}_{\text{centre}}.</me>
<p><em>Symbolically.</em><idx>product-plus-one centre pattern</idx></p>
<me>\boxed{Ax^2+(AC+1)x+C\equiv(Ax+1)(x+C)}</me>
</paragraphs>
<example xml:id="af3-revisit-product-expansion"><title>Revisit — expansion</title>
<statement><p>Expand <m>(5x+1)(x+7)</m>.</p></statement>
<solution><me>\begin{aligned}(5x+1)(x+7)&amp;\equiv5x^2+35x+x+7\\&amp;\equiv5x^2+36x+7.\end{aligned}</me><p>Notice that <m>5\cdot7+1=36</m>.</p></solution></example>
<example xml:id="af3-revisit-product-reverse"><title>Recognise and reverse — factorisation</title>
<statement><p>Factorise <m>6x^2+43x+7</m>.</p></statement>
<solution><p><em>Recognise.</em> The product of the end coefficients, increased by one, is <m>6\cdot7+1=43</m>.</p>
<p><em>Reverse.</em></p><me>\boxed{6x^2+43x+7\equiv(6x+1)(x+7)}</me><p>Expand to check.</p></solution></example>
<exercise xml:id="af3-revisit-product-practice"><title>Product-plus-one centre practice</title>
  <statement><p>Factorise each expression by recognising the pattern. Write your answer as an identity.</p><ol>
    <li><p><m>3x^2+25x+8</m></p></li>
    <li><p><m>7x^2+36x+5</m></p></li>
    <li><p><m>19x^2+210x+11</m></p></li>
    <li><p><m>17x^2+1412x+83</m></p></li>
    <li><p><m>ax^2+(ab+1)x+b</m></p></li>
    <li><p><m>a^2x^2+(a^2b+1)x+b</m></p></li>
  </ol></statement>
  <answer><ol>
    <li><p><m>3x^2+25x+8\equiv(3x+1)(x+8)</m></p></li>
    <li><p><m>7x^2+36x+5\equiv(7x+1)(x+5)</m></p></li>
    <li><p><m>19x^2+210x+11\equiv(19x+1)(x+11)</m></p></li>
    <li><p><m>17x^2+1412x+83\equiv(17x+1)(x+83)</m></p></li>
    <li><p><m>ax^2+(ab+1)x+b\equiv(ax+1)(x+b)</m></p></li>
    <li><p><m>a^2x^2+(a^2b+1)x+b\equiv(a^2x+1)(x+b)</m></p></li>
  </ol></answer>
</exercise>
<example xml:id="af3-revisit-two-arrangements"><title>3. Compare — same ends, different centres</title>
<statement><p>Factorise <m>5x^2+12x+7</m> and <m>5x^2+36x+7</m>. Identify the pattern in each case.</p></statement>
<solution><p>Sum-centre: <m>5+7=12</m>.</p><me>5x^2+12x+7\equiv(5x+7)(x+1).</me>
<p>Product-plus-one centre: <m>5\cdot7+1=36</m>.</p><me>5x^2+36x+7\equiv(5x+1)(x+7).</me></solution></example>
<p>These are special cases of quadratic factorisation, not universal rules. Sometimes the patterns coincide, for example when an end coefficient is 1. Check for a common factor first, and always verify your factorisation by expanding.</p>
<p>Use the <xref ref="af3-yardsticks-interactive-figure"/> to investigate the same relationship with your own numbers. The <url href="https://ronandownes.github.io/apps/apps/algebra/af3-01-unit-factor/index.html" visual="inline, display and area-model app"/> lets you revisit the original unit-factor case and then switch to factorisation.</p>
</section>
<section xml:id="algebra-factorisation-practice">
    <title>Worked Example and Practice</title>
    <example xml:id="example-algebra-factorisation">
      <title>Worked example</title>
      <statement><p>Factorise 6x+9.</p></statement>
      <solution><p>The highest common factor is 3, so 6x+9=3(2x+3).</p></solution>
    </example>
    <exercise xml:id="exercise-algebra-factorisation">
      <statement><p>Factorise x²+7x+12.</p></statement>
      <answer><p>(x+3)(x+4).</p></answer>
    </exercise>
  </section>
</chapter>
